{
  "$comment": [
    "Cross-language contract: owner filter semantics (PRD #137 / ADR-061).",
    "The shared FILTER_FIELDS registry (app/src/lib/filter-model.ts) projects every",
    "filter two ways from one definition: a DuckDB SQL WHERE body (composeOwnerWhere)",
    "and a JS runtime predicate (matchesOwner). This fixture pins both against the",
    "same synthetic owners so the two projections can never silently disagree.",
    "",
    "Consumed by:",
    "  - app/tests/unit/contract-filter-semantics.test.ts (vitest): asserts",
    "      composeOwnerWhere(filter) === case.where  (the generated SQL is current)",
    "      and matchesOwner over `owners` === case.expected_ids  (the JS predicate).",
    "  - tests/test_contract_filter_semantics.py (pytest): builds a DuckDB table from",
    "      `owners` (typed by `owner_columns`), executes case.where, and asserts the",
    "      selected ids === case.expected_ids  (the SQL, run in a real engine).",
    "",
    "`where` is the EXACT string composeOwnerWhere emits today; vitest asserts it",
    "byte-for-byte, so a generator change forces a fixture edit (and the Python side",
    "then runs the new SQL). `expected_ids` is the language-neutral ground truth both",
    "engines must reproduce. An empty `where` ('') means no constraint (all rows).",
    "",
    "These encode TODAY'S behavior (verified: SQL and predicate agree on every case).",
    "Adding a filter field or case extends this file; do not change semantics here.",
    "Scope note: the parcel registry (app/src/lib/filters.ts) is SQL-only (no JS",
    "predicate), so it is not part of this two-engine parity fixture."
  ],
